00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.300
A TWITTER ARMY:
I don’t think you realize how crazy Saudi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:10.380
Arabia’s propaganda room is. 40% of all tweets&nbsp;
from the Arab World come from Saudi Arabia much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:16.920
of which comes from this single room. Using this&nbsp;
army, Saudi Arabia’s crown prince, Mohammed Bin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:25.380
Salman or MBS is able to push whatever story he&nbsp;
wants to the absolute top. #VISION_2030. Trending.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:32.700
#Riyadh_rising. Trending. #MBS_Rocks. Trending.&nbsp;
Not only does this room push certain narratives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:38.640
to the top but it also pushes opposing narratives&nbsp;
to the bottom. If you have any sort of relavance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.840
within the Arab world and you post something&nbsp;
that’s not in the Saudi family’s best interest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:49.500
well then, this army is gonna flood your post with&nbsp;
negative comments and dislikes. We may even see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:55.140
these trolls under this video. At first, Twitter&nbsp;
tried to take down this army by deleting thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.140 --> 00:01:02.160
of their fake accounts. At one point, they had&nbsp;
deleted as many as 88,000 accounts but eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:08.580
even Twitter gave up. The problem is that these&nbsp;
tweets aren’t coming from bots. We’re talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:14.220
about hundreds of real people working full time&nbsp;
on this effort making it extremely difficult to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:19.800
identity. And ever since Elon bought Twitter,&nbsp;
all hopes of this army being taken down have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:26.160
been completely crushed. You see, Elon didn’t&nbsp;
buyout Twitter single handedly. He had several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:32.340
investors pitch in and his biggest investor was&nbsp;
none other than Saudi Prince Alwaleed. With no one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:36.420
else willing to fight against this propaganda,&nbsp;
the Saudi people have taken this matter into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:42.840
their own hands through a movement called the bee&nbsp;
movement. The idea is pretty simple. Just do the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:46.980
exact same thing that Saudi Arabia is doing&nbsp;
but with the might of the Saudi population.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:53.760
Surely a few hundred trolls had no chance against&nbsp;
a country with millions of people right? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:59.640
in theory yes, but Saudi Arabia isn’t&nbsp;
exactly putting up a fair fight. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:04.140
founder of the movement, Jamal Khashoggi,&nbsp;
has literally been assassinated and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:08.040
country has been arresting leaders of&nbsp;
the bee movement left and right. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:13.560
join me as we explore Saudi Arabia’s quest to&nbsp;
extend their dictatorship into the online world.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.020
A PRIVATE KINGDOM:&nbsp;

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:26.040
Taking a look back, the online space in Saudi&nbsp;
Arabia wasn’t always so animated. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:31.620
the Saudi people didn’t even get internet till&nbsp;
1999 because the kingdom was concerned about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:38.280
exactly this. Online protests, the spread of&nbsp;
dissenting ideas, and a more powerful societal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:44.760
voice. But by 2000, the internet was getting so&nbsp;
big that continuing to ignore it would simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:50.640
leave the country behind. So, Saudi Arabia&nbsp;
would go ahead and implement a very limited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:56.640
version of the internet. Saudi internet was to be&nbsp;
regulated by the King Abdulaziz City for Science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:04.140
and Technology or KACST. KACST was responsible for&nbsp;
monitoring all internet traffic coming into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:10.020
country and ensuring that Saudi values and beliefs&nbsp;
were protected. To be honest, it’s not clear how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:15.360
effective this organization was in actually&nbsp;
regulating internet traffic. But, its mere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:21.960
existence itself was enough to deter people from&nbsp;
doing anything sus. After all, you didn’t want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:27.840
see what was on the other side of being caught.&nbsp;
So, people primarily just used the internet for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:33.600
research and educational purposes as opposed&nbsp;
to social media or entertainment. This made the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:39.060
kingdom rather closed off even though they were&nbsp;
technically connected to the internet but that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:44.160
wasn’t a problem. In fact, that’s exactly what&nbsp;
the kingdom wanted. They didn’t care about western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:49.680
perception or influencing the global narrative. As&nbsp;
long as the oil business was booming and they had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:55.620
the Saudi people in line, that’s all they cared&nbsp;
about. But all of this would change on January 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.620 --> 00:04:03.240
2010. This day marked a monumental day for their&nbsp;
neighbors: the UAE as this was the grand opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:10.620
of the Burj Khalifa. At first glance, it was just&nbsp;
a another vanity tower but Dubai had grand plans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:15.540
for this building. Dubai was going to use this&nbsp;
pointy structure to gain world wide relevance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:21.360
through social media, travel, airlines, and even&nbsp;
movies. I mean who could forget the famous scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:26.160
were Tom Cruise scales the side of the Burj&nbsp;
Khalifa in Mission Impossible. This was by no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:32.160
means Dubai’s first PR stunt. They had been trying&nbsp;
to shape global perception for decades because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:38.280
reality was that Dubai was a broke bum. They were&nbsp;
one of the only middle eastern cities that didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:44.220
have oil, so they were trying to build a tourist&nbsp;
hub which wasn’t working out all that great. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:49.380
fact, they literally went bankrupt trying to build&nbsp;
the Burj Khalifa and Abu Dhabi had to bail them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:54.840
out. But, fortunately for Dubai, the Burj Khalifa&nbsp;
garnered more attention than they could have ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.840 --> 00:05:01.200
dreamed of. Suddenly, everyone knew about the&nbsp;
Palm Islands, the Burj Al Arab, Atlantis The Palm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:07.740
the lavish malls, the lamborghini police, and of&nbsp;
course Emirates and their A380s. Almost overnight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:14.280
Dubai’s outlandish dreams of becoming a tourist&nbsp;
paradise became a reality. And all of their fake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:20.280
fortunes became real fortunes. Saudi Arabia&nbsp;
didn’t really care about fortunes as Dubai’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:26.460
wealth was still peanuts in comparison to Saudi&nbsp;
wealth. But what did catch Saudi attention was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:32.880
Dubai’s oil independence. Less than 1% of&nbsp;
Dubai’s GDP was coming from oil. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:39.120
nearly half of Saudi Arabia’s GDP was coming&nbsp;
from oil. It was time for something to change.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:46.620
A CAUTIONARY TALE:&nbsp;

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:53.040
While all of this was unfolding MBS was barely&nbsp;
24 years old, but he already had the ears of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:59.340
country’s most powerful leaders. He held several&nbsp;
roles as a special advisor and a personal aide and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:05.880
to him, the future of Saudi Arabia was clear. He&nbsp;
had to free Saudi Arabia from oil. He was going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:11.040
westernize the country, let in tourists in for the&nbsp;
first time, and revolutionize western perception.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:17.640
But before he did all that, he would be met with&nbsp;
a harsh reminder of just what he was up against.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:24.600
Between 2010 and 2012, the Middle East would be&nbsp;
rocked by a series of uprisings called the Arab&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:29.520
Spring. Essentially, a bunch of authoritarian&nbsp;
Middle Eastern countries would see massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:34.740
protests regarding human rights violations,&nbsp;
corruption, and wealth inequality. The protests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:39.840
started in Tunisia but they would quickly&nbsp;
spread to Libya, Egypt, Yemen, Syria, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:46.200
Bahrain. And do you know what was central to all&nbsp;
of this? The internet. The usage of social media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:50.820
doubled amongst these countries throughout the&nbsp;
Arab Spring. Social media was how activists were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:57.120
able to communicate, organize, and retaliate. In&nbsp;
fact, 9 out of 10 Egyptians and Tunisians admitted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:02.220
that they were using Facebook to organize protests&nbsp;
and spread awareness. The scariest part for these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:08.640
dictators was that all of this was actually&nbsp;
working. In early 2011, Tunisia’s president&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:15.180
of 23 years, Ben Ali, would run away to Saudi&nbsp;
Arabia. Meanwhile, in Yemen, the president would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:21.180
be nearly assassinated before he too would run&nbsp;
away to Saudi Arabia and cede power. This quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:26.820
became a trend as the leaders of Egypt and Libya&nbsp;
would also end up resigining. The Middle East was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:32.880
in turmoil and many of its leaders were turning&nbsp;
to Saudi Arabia for help. And Saudi Arabia would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:39.540
indeed step in. In March of 2011, Saudi Arabia and&nbsp;
the UAE would send forces to Bahrain to occupy the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:45.600
country and silence the protests. But, the UAE&nbsp;
and Saudi Arabia weren’t able to be the saviors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:51.120
for long as protests started erupting in their own&nbsp;
countries. These guys wouldn’t be messing around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:56.760
though. They had seen several of their peers get&nbsp;
overthrown by similar uprisings, so they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:03.120
put their foot down almost immediately. The UAE&nbsp;
would go out and arrest 94 of the most influential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:07.740
journalists, lawyers, teachers, and student&nbsp;
activists and throw them in a secret prison.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:14.340
Some of these individuals are still in prison to&nbsp;
this day. Meanwhile, Saudi Arabia’s military would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:20.100
squash protests using brute force. Things were&nbsp;
looking worse than ever. Throughout much of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:25.320
Middle East, infrastructure had been demolished,&nbsp;
supply chains had been disrupted, inflation was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:31.500
out of control, and poverty was reaching record&nbsp;
highs. And in the end, it was these internal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:36.540
forces that would finally put an end to the&nbsp;
Arab Spring. As it became harder and harder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:42.060
to just get by, people slowly lost the ability to&nbsp;
fight even if they still had the will to fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:48.840
and pretty soon, the Arab Spring would turn&nbsp;
into the Arab Winter. Saudi Arabia had come out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:54.240
victorious but MBS had seen first hand just how&nbsp;
dangerous the internet could be to authoritative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.240 --> 00:09:01.140
regimes. And let’s just say, MBS kept this in&nbsp;
mind when he became the crown prince in 2017.

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:03.420
A PROPAGANDA MACHINE:&nbsp;

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:13.560
MBS was set on westernizing the country.&nbsp;
He had this massive plan called Vision 2030&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:17.880
that was supposed to free Saudi Arabia&nbsp;
of its oil dependence. And honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:23.880
its actually a pretty fine plan. He was going to&nbsp;
invest their oil wealth into health, education,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:30.720
infrastructure, recreation, and tourism but&nbsp;
this of course came with a caveat. He wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:35.940
revolutionize Saudi Arabia without ceding power.&nbsp;
And to make sure that he didn’t lose control,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:41.400
he was going to build up an online army to&nbsp;
enforce his rule. But before he could do all this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:47.880
he first had to raise money. Vision 2030 entailed&nbsp;
spending hundreds of billions every single year so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:54.900
he needed some pretty deep pockets. And who better&nbsp;
to get this from than Saudi’s elite. In late 2017,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.900 --> 00:10:00.960
MBS would round up the country’s 400 most powerful&nbsp;
people in a Ritz Carlton hotel and he would turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:05.460
the hotel into a jail. He basically told the elite&nbsp;
that they could leave as soon as they admitted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:10.920
that they were quote on quote “corrupt” and paid&nbsp;
up. Were these people actually corrupt? Probably.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:17.340
Did MBS actually care about their corruption&nbsp;
though? Probably not. This was simply a masterful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:23.400
way to not only raise money but show the kingdom&nbsp;
who was really in control. And let’s just say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:31.020
he raised a lot of money. Over $100 billion&nbsp;
to be precise. This means that MBS milked a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:36.780
cool quarter billion from each person. Not&nbsp;
a bad day’s work but that was just phase 1.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:42.360
Phase 2 of his fundraising effort consisted of&nbsp;
taking their national oil company, Saudi Aramco,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:48.120
public in the world’s largest IPO with a&nbsp;
valuation of $2 trillion. By this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:53.640
MBS had the country’s wealth and leaders under&nbsp;
control. The next step was keeping the country’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:59.880
people in line despite the new freedoms. Enter&nbsp;
the Twitter Army. As we touched on in the intro,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:04.620
MBS would straight up create an entire Twitter&nbsp;
battle station with the sole intent of promoting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:09.720
his agenda across social media platforms. And&nbsp;
given how much manpower he’s dedicated to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:15.000
effort, it’s not surprising that it was quite&nbsp;
effective. Everything that was trending on Arab&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:24.780
Twitter was exactly what MBS wanted to trend. #1&nbsp;
Vision 2030. #2 Riyadh Rising. #3 MBS Rocks. #6&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.780 --> 00:11:31.110
vision2030. #7 CrownPrince. Shout out to the Ace&nbsp;
Family though, they were literally able to out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:36.240
perform state sponsored propaganda. Anyway, the&nbsp;
Saudi people would start referring this state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:42.540
army as the flies and from that we would see a&nbsp;
new movement pop up called the bees. The bees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:47.340
would do the exact same thing as the flies but&nbsp;
instant of pushing state sponsored propaganda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:52.560
the bees would push for social awareness and&nbsp;
political reform. The first viral hashtag&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:57.600
from the bees was “What do you know about the&nbsp;
bees” initially posted by the movements founder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:03.180
Jamal Khashoggi. But just a few weeks after&nbsp;
this post went viral, Jamal Khashoggi would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:09.480
be assassinated. The Saudis probably that this&nbsp;
would finally put an end to this movement but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:15.120
it simply made it far stronger as it garnered&nbsp;
international attention and sympathy. This did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:19.980
lead to Saudi Arabia backing down for a little&nbsp;
as they laid low avoiding the spotlight. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:25.680
there’s still a long road ahead as in the end,&nbsp;
the Saudi people are directly fighting against a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:31.620
state sponsored propaganda machine backed by one&nbsp;
of the wealthiest countries on earth. All that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:36.720
wealth may not last for long though. In fact,&nbsp;
there’s insider evidence that Saudi Arabia may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:41.340
very well be lying about their oil reserves.&nbsp;
